  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nThrough tonight: continued dry with warmer than normal highs and \nseasonal lows today and tonight. Early morning surface analysis \nstill had a >1024m high centered over the Ohio valley. This surface \nhigh will continue to ridge back to the southwest across our CWA \nthrough tonight. This will result in a light northeast to east low \nlevel flow and essentially keep the Gulf closed. Early morning \nsatellite imagery/RAP analysis showed the circulation around a \n590dam ridge from the central Gulf ridging to the northeast across \nour southeast zones and over the southern Appalachians. The resulting \nsouthwest flow aloft across the western half of our CWA will bring a \nfew mid and high level clouds but no rain. The few high clouds will \ndue little to limit heating as wl top out several degrees warmer \nthan on Monday. The warm afternoon temperatures will combine with \nour dry airmass to result in critically low afternoon minimum \nrelative humidity that wl maintain elevated fire danger concerns. \nThe continued relatively light wind will mitigate Red Flag Warning \ncriteria. /22/\n\nWednesday through next Monday...Quiet weather, dry conditions, with \ncool pleasant mornings and warm afternoons, will continue through \nFriday.  Over the weekend, a shortwave associated with a mid/upper \nlevel low pressure system lifting northeast through the plains into \nthe Great Lakes region, will result in showers and a few \nthunderstorms moving through the forecast area from mainly Saturday \nafternoon through Sunday morning.  Despite these much needed rain \nchances, neither Saturday nor Sunday will be complete washouts nor \nwill it break the ongoing drought conditions across the area. \nHowever, for locations that are lucky enough to receive any rainfall \nover the weekend, it'll be quite welcomed and appreciated.\n\nRain chances will lessen across the area through the course of \nSunday afternoon as the shortwave exists the region.  In the wake of \nit, a slightly drier air mass will advect into the forecast area \nSunday night into Monday.  Temperatures will remain warm and in the \nlow to middle 80s Monday afternoon, with lows Monday night cooling \ninto the mid 50s to lower 60s. /19/",
